Kissat 4.0.4 in ABC

Upstream: https://github.com/arminbiere/kissat
Release: rel-4.0.4 (8af8e56f174b778aef3aa45af9f739b2a5f492c2)

The solver sources retain ABC's namespace and C++ adaptations, renamed
identifiers and files, portability fixes, timeout callback polling, and
kissat_is_inconsistent API. The stack allocation macros use a typed cast
when compiled as C++. The standalone Kissat application is not included.
An empty stack.c is retained for legacy ABC project files.

ABC builds with compact references, fixed options, no proof output, and
quiet output, as selected in global.h. ABC retains its existing fastel=1
default; upstream 4.0.4 defaults to fastel=0. Neither setting wins all tested
exact-synthesis cases, and enabling it performed better overall in the
upgrade comparisons. The new factoring delay follows upstream 4.0.4.
With KISSAT_NOPTIONS, kissat_set_option does not change runtime settings.
For optional runtime-options builds, kissat_reserve skips fixed and eliminated
variables when tumble=0. This supports reserving after unit clauses without
reactivating variables that propagation has already fixed.

ABC-local fast elimination optimization

fastel.c contains a local early-rejection guard in fast_forward_subsumed.
For a large clause in the occurrence list of a non-false literal, the subset
test must fail when that literal is unmarked. The guard avoids loading and
scanning that clause. It leaves the binary-clause check unchanged, and does
not reject on a false literal because the subset test skips false literals.
It remains active with ABC's fast-elimination default.

This optimization is independent of casexact. Its benefit depends on how
much time a workload spends in fast forward subsumption; compact CNFs may
show little benefit. A representative preprocessing-heavy ABC workload is:

  %gen "mcomp;i5a,b;o10z=a*b"; %blast; &put; cone -O 9; clp; st;
  lutexact -K 6 -M 2 -kr

When refreshing the vendored Kissat sources, check whether this guard or an
equivalent upstream change is present. Preserve or re-evaluate the local
optimization explicitly. The change has not been submitted upstream.

ABC-local propagation optimizations

Blocking watches are two-word records. When their vector has two adjacent
free slots, append both words together; otherwise use the original append
path. This preserves allocation and watch order while avoiding a second
capacity check and vector update on the common path.

The literal being propagated provides a lower bound on an implication's
assignment level. When this equals the current decision level, no reason
scan is needed. Other reason scans stop upon reaching the same upper bound.
Debug builds retain the full scan and check the shortcut against it. These
changes preserve the solver's search and do not change runtime options.
